   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1/ Ratings in human rights index
2/ Level of public trust towards state institutions</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2020">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">World Justice Project Rule of Law Index 2020 - 0.61 points; Freedom in the World Report - 61 points
Trust in Patrol police &#x2013; 64%, PDO &#x2013; 57%, MIA &#x2013; 42%, common courts &#x2013; 35%, PDPI -26%</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening the capacity of national non-governmental counterparts to protect human rights through targeted trainings and other capacity-building activities;
Creating the environment (tools and opportunities) for PwDs to participate in decision-making through knowledge building, empowering PwDs and preparing instruments for government agencies. 
Contribute to the capacity building of national governmental institutions to implement the national regulations and policies aimed at improving the human rights situation of vulnerable groups, including PwDs, LGBTQI+, national minorities, etc., through knowledge building, sensitization and preparing manuals/regulations. 
Support the finalization of the National Human Rights Strategy and continue policy-lobbying on its approval, as well as the advancement of the rights of vulnerable groups.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">In partnership with OHCHR, the Project strengthened the capacity of national non-governmental counterparts to protect human rights resulting in broader acceptance of human rights principles and increased access to justice for persons with disabilities and other vulnerable groups, including LGBTQI+, national minorities, etc. In total, over ten trainings were conducted. Furthermore, the Project supported the legal profession in providing effective legal remedies to the most vulnerable. 
To accelerate the transformation towards more inclusive models based on the LNOB principle, the Project supported increased resilience and participation of PwDs in decision-making, benefiting over 100,000 of them with increased access to Government programs and the ability to participate in policymaking through consultative platforms. A manual for disability-inclusive budgeting at the local level was developed. The Project supported PwDs to participate in various government platforms, including consultative councils and committees under the Government and Ministries. 
The Project contributed to the capacity building of national governmental institutions to implement the national regulations and policies aimed at improving the human rights situation of vulnerable groups. The target audience was employees of law enforcement agencies (Ministry of Internal Affairs, Prosecutors Office), Revenue Service of the Ministry of Finance, National Forensic Bureau, and social workers. In total, over 700 government employees have a better capacity to address human rights grievances and prevent violations of human rights. 
Improved regulatory framework on human rights issues is another result of the 2022 activities; the Project supported the development and approval by the Government of the National Human Rights Strategy. Furthermore, with the Projects support, the following documents were elaborate on and approved:
&#x2022;	Guideline for social workers on Assessing the needs of people with psychosocial needs
&#x2022;	recommendations for social workers on communication with persons with psychosocial needs
&#x2022;	The Strategy of the Public Defenders Office on monitoring the human rights situation of persons with disabilities. 
The Project created a space for discussion between the representative of Tbilisi Pride and the Ministry of Internal Affairs aimed at the peaceful and safe conduct of the Tbilisi Pride 2022 events in Tbilisi.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
